WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.866
Level 2 contains the systems that provide supervisory control and visibility for a process area.

00:00:05.866 --> 00:00:08.557
Human-machine interfaces, local operator stations, alarm services,

00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:12.401
and area-level control servers are commonly associated with this level.

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:17.398
They translate controller data into information that operators can understand and act upon.

00:00:17.398 --> 00:00:20.858
An HMI displays measurements, equipment state, trends, and alarms.

00:00:20.858 --> 00:00:26.240
Depending on authorization and design, it may also allow an operator to start equipment,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.084
acknowledge alarms, adjust a setpoint, or select an operating mode.

00:00:30.084 --> 00:00:33.543
The HMI does not replace the controller's real-time logic,

00:00:33.543 --> 00:00:37.003
but it can influence how that logic is used.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:41.616
Level 2 compromise can affect operations even when field controllers remain intact.

00:00:41.616 --> 00:00:45.076
Operators may lose visibility, see misleading values, miss alarms,

00:00:45.076 --> 00:00:47.766
or issue commands based on untrustworthy information.

00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:52.379
A widespread workstation outage may cause a facility to enter a safer

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.992
manual mode or stop production because continued operation cannot be supervised confidently.

00:00:56.992 --> 00:00:59.683
Controls should protect both availability and integrity.

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:03.143
Limit administrative access, separate engineering privileges from routine operation,

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:05.834
maintain recoverable configurations, monitor communications with controllers,

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:08.140
and restrict pathways from higher levels.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:14.291
Endpoint protections must be tested so they do not disrupt required applications or introduce uncontrolled reboots.

00:01:14.291 --> 00:01:18.519
Incident planning should define how operators recognize loss of trusted visibility,

00:01:18.519 --> 00:01:21.979
what local indications remain, and which actions are safe.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:27.360
Level 2 shows why cybersecurity cannot focus only on whether control logic was changed.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.820
Reliable operator awareness is itself a critical control function.
